-- title:
--   Corollary 2.1, Parts 1–3 — Fejér monotonicity, vanishing residual and weak convergence of Algorithm 1
-- statement:
--   Let $H$ be a real Hilbert space, $A, B : H \to 2^H$ maximal monotone, $C : H \to H$ $\beta$-cocoercive with $\beta > 0$, and $J_A = J_{\gamma A}$, $J_B = J_{\gamma B}$ the resolvents. Let $T$ be the operator of Eq. (1.2) and suppose $\operatorname{Fix} T \neq \emptyset$. Let $\varepsilon \in (0,1)$, $\gamma \in (0, 2\beta\varepsilon)$, $\alpha = 1/(2-\varepsilon)$, and let $(\lambda_j)_{j\ge0} \subseteq (0, 1/\alpha)$ satisfy
--   $$\sum_{i=0}^\infty \tau_i = \infty, \qquad \tau_k = \lambda_k(1-\lambda_k) + \frac{\lambda_k(1-\alpha)}{\alpha}.$$
--   Let $(z^j)_{j \ge 0}$ be generated by Algorithm 1 from an arbitrary $z^0 \in H$. Then:
--
--   1. for every $z^* \in \operatorname{Fix} T$, the sequence $(\|z^j - z^*\|)_{j\ge0}$ is nonincreasing;
--   2. the fixed-point residual $(\|Tz^j - z^j\|)_{j\ge0}$ is nonincreasing and converges to $0$;
--   3. $(z^j)_{j \ge 0}$ converges weakly to a fixed point $z^*$ of $T$, and $J_B(z^*)$ is a solution of (1.1), i.e. $0 \in (A + B + C)J_B(z^*)$.
--
--   This is the Krasnosel'skiĭ–Mann convergence theory applied to the averaged operator $T$; its weak limit $z^*$ is the point around which Theorem 2.1 is organised.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The corollary as printed also requires $\alpha = 1/(2-\varepsilon) < 2\beta/(4\beta-\gamma)$; since $\gamma < 2\beta\varepsilon$ is equivalent to $2\beta/(4\beta-\gamma) < 1/(2-\varepsilon)$, that comparison contradicts the stepsize condition (it is a typo for ">") and is not a hypothesis here. The printed $\tau_k = (1 - \lambda_k/\alpha)\lambda_k/\alpha$ is replaced by the $\tau_k$ of the proof of Theorem 2.1 (p. 836), which is at least as large on $(0, 1/\alpha)$, so the hypothesis $\sum\tau_i = \infty$ is weaker. Divergence of the series is stated as divergence of its partial sums to $+\infty$.
-- source:
--   Davis and Yin, A Three-Operator Splitting Scheme and its Optimization Applications, Set-Valued Var. Anal. 25 (2017), https://doi.org/10.1007/s11228-017-0421-z, pp. 834-835, Corollary 2.1, Parts 1-3

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ThreeOpSplitting_Convergence_MonotoneOperators
import Definitions.Def_ThreeOpSplitting_Convergence_WeakConvergence
import Definitions.Def_ThreeOpSplitting_Convergence_ThreeOperatorIteration

open Filter Topology

namespace ThreeOpSplitting.Convergence

/-- Davis–Yin, Corollary 2.1, Parts 1–3 (pp. 834–835): Krasnosel'skiĭ–Mann convergence of
Algorithm 1. The printed comparison `α < 2β/(4β - γ)` is not a hypothesis (it contradicts
`γ < 2βε`), and `τ_k` is the coefficient used in the proof (p. 836). -/
theorem corollary_2_1 {H : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup H] [InnerProductSpace ℝ H]
    [CompleteSpace H]
    (A B : H → Set H) (C : H → H) (β γ ε : ℝ) (JA JB : H → H) (lam : ℕ → ℝ) (z0 : H)
    (hA : IsMaximalMonotone A) (hB : IsMaximalMonotone B)
    (hβ : 0 < β) (hC : IsCocoercive β C)
    (hJA : IsResolvent γ A JA) (hJB : IsResolvent γ B JB)
    (hε0 : 0 < ε) (hε1 : ε < 1) (hγ0 : 0 < γ) (hγ : γ < 2 * β * ε)
    (hlam : ∀ j, 0 < lam j ∧ lam j < 1 / alpha ε)
    (hτ : Tendsto (fun n => ∑ i ∈ Finset.range n, tau ε (lam i)) atTop atTop)
    (hFix : (Function.fixedPoints (threeOp γ JA JB C)).Nonempty) :
    let T := threeOp γ JA JB C
    let z := zSeq γ JA JB C lam z0
    (∀ zs ∈ Function.fixedPoints T, Antitone (fun j => ‖z j - zs‖)) ∧
      Antitone (fun j => ‖T (z j) - z j‖) ∧
      Tendsto (fun j => ‖T (z j) - z j‖) atTop (𝓝 0) ∧
      ∃ zs ∈ Function.fixedPoints T, WeakTendsto z zs ∧ JB zs ∈ zer (opSum A B C) := by sorry

end ThreeOpSplitting.Convergence
